WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.266 --> 00:00:01.701 size:84% position:42%
-I want to talk about
Sam Nunberg real quick.

00:00:01.701 --> 00:00:06.072 align:start size:94% position:6%
He -- an ex-Trump aide who had
sort of, you know,

00:00:06.072 --> 00:00:07.941 align:start size:94% position:6%
kind of dominated
cable news for...

00:00:07.941 --> 00:00:09.075 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah.
-...a 24-hour period

00:00:09.075 --> 00:00:12.412
when he -- melted down
maybe is a little unkind.

00:00:12.412 --> 00:00:15.215
But he was off the rails
as far as somebody talking

00:00:15.215 --> 00:00:19.519 align:start size:94% position:6%
about his time in front
of the Mueller investigation.

00:00:19.519 --> 00:00:21.688
Nunberg was the one who brought
you to Mar-a-Lago.

00:00:21.688 --> 00:00:23.690 align:start size:94% position:6%
He was the one who thought it
was a good idea for you

00:00:23.690 --> 00:00:25.025 align:start size:94% position:6%
to travel with Trump then.
-Yes.

00:00:25.025 --> 00:00:26.893 align:start size:94% position:6%
-And so then you wrote this
unflattering piece,

00:00:26.893 --> 00:00:28.628 align:start size:94% position:6%
and Trump was not happy
with Nunberg.

00:00:28.628 --> 00:00:30.897 size:81% position:41%
-Yeah, Trump fired him
because of the piece,

00:00:30.897 --> 00:00:34.601
then rehired him later
and then fired him again.

00:00:34.601 --> 00:00:36.569 align:start size:94% position:6%
The thing about Sam Nunberg
that I realized

00:00:36.569 --> 00:00:39.105 align:start size:94% position:6%
when I was kind of
stuck at Mar-a-Lago with him

00:00:39.105 --> 00:00:42.776 align:start size:94% position:6%
was that he is part of
this kind of rotating cast

00:00:42.776 --> 00:00:44.744 align:start size:94% position:6%
of, like, kind of
weird characters

00:00:44.744 --> 00:00:46.813 align:start size:94% position:6%
that Trump has always
liked to have around him

00:00:46.813 --> 00:00:50.116 align:start size:94% position:6%
who are all sort of aspiring
mini Trumps, you know?

00:00:50.116 --> 00:00:51.985 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah.
-Like, they dress like him.

00:00:51.985 --> 00:00:56.122
They kind of take on his affect
and his, like,

00:00:56.122 --> 00:00:57.557 size:88% position:44% line:92%
his linguistic stylings.

00:00:57.557 --> 00:00:59.426 align:start size:94% position:6%
Like, they'll use
the same words as him.

00:00:59.426 --> 00:01:02.328
And they all try to mimic
his kind of performance.

00:01:02.328 --> 00:01:05.632 align:start size:94% position:6%
And it's funny because, like,
you know, he fires people

00:01:05.632 --> 00:01:07.233 line:92%
and hires people all the time.

00:01:07.233 --> 00:01:08.435 line:92%
But even now in the White House,

00:01:08.435 --> 00:01:09.402 align:start size:94% position:6%
he keeps people
like that around.

00:01:09.402 --> 00:01:13.139 align:start size:94% position:6%
And he sets up this kind of --
the environment he fosters

00:01:13.139 --> 00:01:14.441 align:start size:94% position:6%
is sort of like
"The Hunger Games,"

00:01:14.441 --> 00:01:18.378
where he puts all -- he pits
his aides against each other

00:01:18.378 --> 00:01:21.448 align:start size:94% position:6%
and kind of feels like
whoever is able to survive

00:01:21.448 --> 00:01:24.084 size:81% position:41%
and emerge victorious,
that's the best person

00:01:24.084 --> 00:01:24.818 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
to have around him.

00:01:24.818 --> 00:01:27.087 align:start size:94% position:6%
-And so the reports
obviously of chaos --

00:01:27.087 --> 00:01:28.421 align:start size:94% position:6%
well, it seems like
what you're saying

00:01:28.421 --> 00:01:31.825 align:start size:94% position:6%
is he likes chaos,
but he does not care for

00:01:31.825 --> 00:01:33.193 size:78% position:39% line:92%
the reports of chaos.

00:01:33.193 --> 00:01:34.127 size:84% position:42% line:92%
-Right. He likes chaos.

00:01:34.127 --> 00:01:36.362 align:start size:94% position:6%
He just doesn't like the media
calling it chaos, right?

00:01:36.362 --> 00:01:37.697 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Right.
-I think he actually does

00:01:37.697 --> 00:01:41.601
thrive in this kind of --
this chaotic environment.

00:01:41.601 --> 00:01:42.402 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
He fosters it.

00:01:42.402 --> 00:01:44.804
He did at the Trump organization
before he was president.

00:01:44.804 --> 00:01:47.707 line:92%
He does now in the White House.

00:01:47.707 --> 00:01:51.811
But what he doesn't like is when
the media is constantly saying,

00:01:51.811 --> 00:01:53.913 align:start size:94% position:6%
you know, "Another chaotic day
in the White House."

00:01:53.913 --> 00:01:56.983 align:start size:94% position:6%
You know? And your nightly
monologues about --

00:01:56.983 --> 00:01:58.551 align:start size:94% position:6%
he probably doesn't care
for those either.

00:01:58.551 --> 00:02:01.654 align:start size:94% position:6%
But the thing is he does --
it's funny.

00:02:01.654 --> 00:02:04.858 align:start size:94% position:6%
I think he likes the stories
about, you know,

00:02:04.858 --> 00:02:06.726 align:start size:94% position:6%
his people on the brink
of being fired.

00:02:06.726 --> 00:02:08.027
-Yeah.
-Because if you think about it,

00:02:08.027 --> 00:02:10.930 align:start size:94% position:6%
he's always had -- ever since
his "Apprentice" days,

00:02:10.930 --> 00:02:13.133 align:start size:94% position:6%
he's loved --
he doesn't actually like

00:02:13.133 --> 00:02:14.267 line:92%
the act of firing people.

00:02:14.267 --> 00:02:15.535 align:start size:94% position:6%
You know, we've seen reporting
about this.

00:02:15.535 --> 00:02:18.171
He doesn't like sitting down
with the staffer and saying,

00:02:18.171 --> 00:02:19.205 align:start size:94% position:6%
"I'm sorry,
this isn't working out."

00:02:19.205 --> 00:02:22.375
But he likes performing his
authority and his power, right?

00:02:22.375 --> 00:02:24.144 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah.
-He likes, you know --

00:02:24.144 --> 00:02:24.978 line:92%
Even on "The Apprentice,"

00:02:24.978 --> 00:02:27.347 align:start size:94% position:6%
he would practice
the delivery of his line

00:02:27.347 --> 00:02:28.915 align:start size:94% position:6%
and, like,
the swivel of his finger

00:02:28.915 --> 00:02:31.017 align:start size:94% position:6%
when he, like, pointed
to the person being fired.

00:02:31.017 --> 00:02:34.687 align:start size:94% position:6%
And he still does that.
Like, he likes the performance

00:02:34.687 --> 00:02:35.889 align:start size:94% position:6%
of, like,
"Look how powerful I am.

00:02:35.889 --> 00:02:38.558
"Everyone's fate is in my hand,
and I get to decide

00:02:38.558 --> 00:02:41.494
which subordinates will survive
and which ones won't."

00:02:41.494 --> 00:02:43.563 align:start size:94% position:6%
All of that stuff is --
he loves.

00:02:43.563 --> 00:02:46.166 size:81% position:41%
He just doesn't like
when we call it chaos.

00:02:46.166 --> 00:02:48.234 line:92%
-So, and has this been --

00:02:48.234 --> 00:02:52.038 size:88% position:44%
you know, people ask me
all the time for my job,

00:02:52.038 --> 00:02:54.240 align:start size:94% position:6%
has it been
just a fascinating time

00:02:54.240 --> 00:02:55.675 line:92%
to work in Washington, D.C.?

00:02:55.675 --> 00:02:56.876 line:92%
Are you going to look back on it

00:02:56.876 --> 00:02:58.578 align:start size:94% position:6%
and say, "Oh, I'm so happy
I worked there"?

00:02:58.578 --> 00:02:59.979 align:start size:94% position:6%
Or are you going to look back
and say,

00:02:59.979 --> 00:03:01.514 align:start size:94% position:6%
"I wish I had any other job
in the world"?

00:03:01.514 --> 00:03:03.950 size:84% position:42%
-[ Laughs ] No, I mean,
I'm glad that I have,

00:03:03.950 --> 00:03:07.053
you know, this, like, front-row
seat to this historic thing.

00:03:07.053 --> 00:03:09.856
It's just hard because I don't
know how it's going to turn out.

00:03:09.856 --> 00:03:10.690 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah.
-And nobody does.

00:03:10.690 --> 00:03:14.861
And obviously those of us who
got his presidential aspirations

00:03:14.861 --> 00:03:17.597 align:start size:94% position:6%
so wrong are very careful
with predictions.

00:03:17.597 --> 00:03:18.932 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah.
-But maybe we'll look back

00:03:18.932 --> 00:03:22.969 align:start size:94% position:6%
at this time as, like, a great
moment in American history.

00:03:22.969 --> 00:03:25.004 align:start size:94% position:6%
-And maybe we won't.
-Yeah.

00:03:25.004 --> 00:03:25.605 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Laughter ]

00:03:25.605 --> 00:03:28.074
-Here's for -- well, here's
hoping for your and I's sakes

00:03:28.074 --> 00:03:29.375 align:start size:94% position:6%
it's great.
-Yeah, it makes me optimistic.

00:03:29.375 --> 00:03:31.110
-Yeah, it turns out great.
And you and I get statues.

00:03:31.110 --> 00:03:33.213
-Yeah, exactly.
-Thanks so much for being here.

00:03:33.213 --> 00:03:35.081 line:92%
McKay Coppins, everybody.

